{"attack": true, "case": "The target's claim asserts a causal or justificatory link between a theory's test-survival record and its being 'logically closer to the truth.' But verisimilitude, as Popper defines it, is a fixed semantic property of a theory's logical content relative to the truth — it is intrinsic and does not change when a test is run. Severe-test survival is an extrinsic, historical fact about what happened when the theory was confronted with evidence. Since running a test does not alter a theory's logical content, it cannot alter its verisimilitude. Therefore 'because it has withstood more severe tests' cannot be the reason the theory is 'logically closer to the truth.' The only way to connect test-survival to verisimilitude is as an evidential indicator — i.e., an inference from past observational track record to the theory's current truth-likeness. But that is inductive inference by another name, which the claim explicitly purports to avoid. The argument's internal structure thus forces an equivocation: it treats verisimilitude as both an intrinsic logical property (to keep it non-probabilistic and non-inductive) and as something that 'increases' with test survival (to link it to the preference). These are mutually exclusive characterizations, so the defense is internally incoherent regardless of whether the Miller-Tichý formal result applies.", "premise": "Verisimilitude, as a logical-content measure, is an intrinsic semantic property of a theory and is invariant under the historical event of a test being performed; test-survival is an extrinsic historical property. A theory's logical closeness to the truth cannot be caused or increased by a test outcome, only indicated by it, and using it as an indicator is inductive inference.", "premise_evidence": [{"block": "9b36b0954abeefe4", "quote": "We prefer the theory that has withstood more severe tests because it is logically closer to the truth, even if we cannot assign it a higher probability of being true."}, {"block": "9b36b0954abeefe4", "quote": "Surviving severe tests increases our rational expectation of verisimilitude without invoking inductive inference about future success."}], "successor_question": "Can the target specify a non-inductive epistemic rule that connects an extrinsic test-survival record to an intrinsic, content-fixed logical property like verisimilitude without that connection constituting an inference from past evidence to present truth-likeness?"}